/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  curate14_nrtl_butanol_water -- THE REAL WATER + 1-BUTANOL PAIR: BUBBLE
  TEMPERATURES AND MEASURED TIE LINES IN ONE FIT (DEV.md 4c C16, C225, C226).

  The synthetic witness curate13 proves that the machinery recovers a pair it
  was given.  This case runs the same machinery (`kind T_bubble+LLE`) on
  MEASURED data from the NIST/TRC ThermoML Archive (doi 10.18434/mds2-2422,
  the copy on the curator's machine, sha256-verified), and asks the one
  question the synthetic case cannot: does a two-parameter NRTL pair hold the
  gap the measurements show AND the boiling liquid, and does it predict
  studies it was never handed?

  WHAT THE ARCHIVE HOLDS FOR THIS PAIR (found by reading the XML, not by the
  indexer).  No file of the archive is a pure binary water + 1-butanol study.
  Every datum below is a BINARY BLOCK inside a ternary or quaternary study (the
  authors report the binary edge of their system), so: (a) bin/choupo-thermoml
  vle-index finds ONE usable isobaric bubble series (doi 10.1021/je700411p, 15
  interior points) and the heteroazeotrope temperatures of two more; (b) the
  liquid-liquid blocks are not reached by `extract-vle`, so the tie-line files
  were written by a small curator script (kept out of the tree) that reads the
  same XML, pairs the two liquids of each temperature, and converts mass
  fractions to mole fractions (M 74.123 and 18.015 g/mol, said in the file).
  Whether a given binary row was measured by its authors or taken from the
  literature is not recorded in the archive; the files say `reviewStatus
  transcribedNotCheckedAgainstArticle` and the run announces it.

  THE PARTITION, DECLARED BEFORE THE FIRST FIT.
    FIT      bubble temperatures: doi 10.1021/je700411p (15 points, 101.3 kPa);
             tie lines: the five studies that report THREE OR MORE
             temperatures -- 10.1021/je700411p (5), 10.1021/acs.jced.7b00302 (3),
             10.1021/je5007218 (3), 10.1016/j.fluid.2007.12.003 (3),
             10.1021/acs.jced.6b00941 (3): 17 tie lines, 288-362 K.
    HELD OUT bubble temperatures: 10.1016/j.jct.2014.07.021 and
             10.1016/j.jct.2016.04.013 (two heteroazeotrope temperatures each,
             about 365.8 K); tie lines: the eighteen other studies, one or
             two temperatures each (21 tie lines, 288-366 K).  No author name
             of a held-out study appears in a fit study (the archive carries no
             affiliations, so a shared author is the dependence the rule can
             check).
    BANDS    written in `acceptance` below with their origin (maxAAD 0.042 % of
             T, maxLLE 0.01 in mole fraction).
    TRIAGE   `loso_*` fit the pair with ONE tie-line study moved from fit to
             held-out in turn, to see whether the verdict is the property of
             the pair or of one study.

  THE FIT.  NRTL with a_ij = a_ji = 0 and alpha = 0.30 fixed: TWO parameters
  (b_ij, b_ji), i = nButanol, j = water.  Bubble rows are weighted by 1/U (each
  point's own stated expanded uncertainty), tie-line mole fractions by 1/0.005.
  The start (b_ij 0, b_ji 1000 K) splits the liquid at every measured tie line;
  `fit_mixed_start2` starts elsewhere and must land on the same pair.

  WHAT THE RUN FOUND (pinned in `expected`; measured, not argued).  NOT
  VALIDATED, and not promoted: no record for the catalogue was written.
    * fit_mixed: b_ij = -39.14 K, b_ji = 1178.21 K (curate13's synthetic
      generating pair: 150 / 1050); fit_mixed_start2 lands on -39.14 / 1178.21
      from another start.  Identifiable (cond JtJ 49, max|corr| 0.87).
      In-sample: 49 rows (15 bubble + 17 tie lines x 2), reduced chi2 52.
    * Bubble temperatures: every one of the 15 fit points is predicted 0.1-2.9
      K HIGH (the heteroazeotrope region 1.9-2.2 K): the pair that holds the
      gap cannot hold the boiling liquid.  HELD OUT (4 heteroazeotrope points,
      two studies): AAD 1.72 K = 0.47 % against a band of 0.042 % -> MISS.
    * Tie lines: all 21 held-out tie lines are predicted as TWO liquids (288-
      366 K), the measured gap exists in the model.  Held-out AAD in mole
      fraction 0.0165 against 0.01 (water-rich liquid 0.0126, butanol-rich
      liquid 0.0203, recomputed outside the engine: same figures) -> MISS.
      The model makes the butanol solubility in water RISE with T (0.019 at 288
      K to 0.067 at 366 K); the measurements keep it flat (0.016-0.021, a
      shallow minimum near 333 K), which an NRTL with a_ij = a_ji = 0 and one
      alpha cannot draw; the butanol-rich liquid is 0.01-0.06 too rich in
      butanol above 320 K.
    * Leave-one-study-out (4 of the 5 tie-line fit studies; the fifth,
      10.1021/je700411p, is the only bubble series and the engine refuses its
      archive file in both roles): b_ij -35.2 .. -35.6 (-37.0), b_ji 1171-1175;
      held-out LLE AAD 0.012-0.020; every verdict is the same MISS.  The miss
      is the pair's form against these data, not one study's.
    * The vapour model contributes: the catalogue's water Antoine sits 0.5-0.7
      K low at 1 atm (C226 item 7); the declared bubble band is the strictest
      reading of the C16 rule (no pure endpoint in the held-out sets), and it
      would be missed by a perfect pair on this vapour model.  Nothing was
      tuned to change either verdict.
    * Independence: je700411p supplies the bubble series AND five tie lines of
      the fit (the run announces "one publication, counted twice"); the five
      held-out heteroazeotrope temperatures are three values (365.7-366.2 K).

  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED.  The pair lives in this frame: ideal-gas vapour, the
  catalogue's Antoine records (water about 0.5 K low at 1 atm, C226 item 7),
  one pressure for the bubble data (101.3 kPa), LLE data at about atmospheric
  pressure.  Nothing here tests it at another pressure or with another vapour
  model.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

